               "technical":  "With only two trading days, EMA 50/100/200, Bollinger Bands, MACD and RSI 14 (a 0–100 momentum gauge) are all null. There is no trend to read yet, so this is only about price levels. On 19 Nov the stock opened at $111.50, hit the 52-week high of $115.10 and closed at $97.17. On 20 Nov it opened at $89.86, traded between $92.56 and $83.05, and closed at $88.91, down 8.5%. The $83.05 low is also the 52-week low and sits just above the $83 IPO price, the first support. Buyers defended it, and the close was $5.86 off the low. Resistance is $92.56 (the day-two high), then $97.17 (the day-one close). Volume was 399.6M shares against a 628.4M average. That average covers only the two sessions, so day two traded far lighter than the debut: steady selling, not panic. Two lower closes make the short-term tone weak, but the overall posture stays neutral until real indicators exist. RSI 14 needs about three more weeks of data.",
               "fundamental":  "Anthropic is the model layer of the AI value chain. It rents GPUs and TPUs from Nvidia, Google, Amazon and AMD and sells Claude through its API, apps, Claude Code and cloud marketplaces. The amended S-1 shows nine-month 2026 revenue of $33.24B, up ~1,200%, with Q3 about $17B; 79% came from usage fees and 21% from subscriptions. Run-rate revenue was ~$81B at end-October. The operating loss was a modest $1.26B over nine months, while most of the $104B net loss is non-cash fair-value changes. The IPO raised $81.3B in the base deal and about 1.127B shares were sold including the over-allotment, mainly to fund compute. Open questions are customer concentration, compute costs, OpenAI (IPO likely 2027) and Google, and the company\u0027s two 2026 disputes with the US government.",
