               "summary":  "Meta Platforms closed at $560.84 on November 20, 2026, down 5.93% on the day and 28.1% off its $779.82 52-week high. Core advertising remains strong: Q3 revenue jumped 26% year-over-year to $64.60 billion on October 21, with 3.64 billion daily active people. However, $34.91 billion in quarterly capex pushed free cash flow negative (-$2.12 billion), prompting $37.5 billion in October 26 equity/preferred offerings, €9 billion in euro debt, and an October 30 S\u0026P negative outlook. Regulatory fines and November 19 Muse privacy glitches deepened the sell-off, though Meta\u0027s consumer network moat remains intact.",
               "technical":  "Meta\u0027s chart is decisively bearish after Friday\u0027s 5.93% slide to $560.84 on November 20, 2026. Although the three EMAs (exponential moving averages tracking trend) remain stacked bullishly—EMA 50 at $644.76, EMA 100 at $642.27, and EMA 200 at $639.17—price has broken 12%–13% below that entire cluster, turning $639–$645 into heavy overhead resistance alongside October\u0027s $625 forward-offering price. Momentum confirms the downtrend: MACD (a gauge comparing fast and slow trend averages) sits deeply negative at -19.88 below its -16.90 signal line, with a widening -2.98 histogram. However, downside stretch is nearing short-term extremes. Price closed $22.05 beneath the lower Bollinger Band (a 20-day volatility channel spanning $582.89 to $654.24), while RSI 14 (a 0–100 momentum gauge where under 30 is oversold) fell to 30.26. Trading volume of 25.59 million shares topped the 20-day average of 21.66 million by 18%, confirming institutional distribution. Now sitting in the bottom 16% of its $520.26–$779.82 52-week range, META faces major support near $520.26."
